class UpdateRecordDto(TypedDict):
    name: NotRequired[str]
    """Record name relative to the zone: `@` for the zone apex, a label such as `www`, a multi-label name
    such as `_sip._tcp`, or a wildcard such as `*.dev`. Letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, `@` and
    `*` only. A name that already ends in the zone name (`www.example.com`) is taken as complete; a name
    outside the zone is refused with `DNS_RECORD_INVALID`. The name is stored lower-case as an FQDN with
    a trailing dot. Changing it moves this value to the record set of the new name; the record `id`
    stays the same.
    """
    type: NotRequired[Literal["A", "AAAA", "CNAME", "MX", "TXT", "SRV", "NS"]]
    """Record type. Changing it moves this value to the record set of the new type; the record `id` stays
    the same.
    """
    ttl: NotRequired[float]
    """TTL in seconds (60 to 86400). TTL belongs to the record set (all values with the same name and
    type), so a different TTL here changes it for every value in that set. When the name or type
    changes, it is applied to the record set the value moves to.
    """
    target: NotRequired[str]
    """Record value. A: IPv4 address. AAAA: IPv6 address. CNAME, NS, MX, SRV: host name (trailing dot
    optional, underscore labels allowed). TXT: any non-empty text. Surrounding whitespace (spaces, tabs,
    line breaks) is removed before it is checked and stored.
    """
    priority: NotRequired[float]
    """Priority (0 to 65535). Required for MX and SRV. Stored as 0 when omitted, and left out of responses
    when it is 0.
    """
    weight: NotRequired[float]
    """SRV weight (0 to 65535). Required for SRV. Stored as 0 when omitted, and left out of responses when
    it is 0.
    """
    port: NotRequired[float]
    """SRV port (0 to 65535). Required for SRV. Stored as 0 when omitted, and left out of responses when it
    is 0.
    """
    flatten: NotRequired[bool]
    """CNAME only. `true` makes a flattened CNAME: the name server answers A/AAAA queries with the
    addresses of the target. Use it for a CNAME at the zone apex (`@`), where a plain CNAME conflicts
    with the zone NS records. A flattened CNAME may share its name with other types (MX, TXT, NS and so
    on) but not with A or AAAA. Ignored for other types.
    """


